hello I'm Diane Worth I'm professor of  immunology and infectious disease at the  Harvard th Chan School of Public Health  and today I'm talking with Dan Hartmann  of the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation  Dan welcome  well thanks Dianne it's a pleasure to be  here as director of integrated  development what does that mean in the  context of malaria eradication versus  product development so in product  development we are constantly thinking  about how to pull all of the different  pieces together whether it's a clinical  plan a regulatory strategy a  manufacturing plan a toxicology plan  thinking about the sequence of these  various activities and how to do that in  the most efficient cost-effective way as  I look at all of the research being done  in malaria they tend to be one-off you  know we're gonna answer this question  and then we're going to answer the other  question and then the subsequent  question and you have a whole bunch of  questions being answered but how do you  integrate all of those different results  into a cost effective way to eradicate  malaria how do you think about the  combination of various vector control  tools do I need bed nets and IRS and you  know newer vector control tools or can I  substitute in how do you think about  that well if I have an effective vector  control do I need to do mass Drug  Administration and and what type of  population and in what type of setting  you know high intensity low intensity  indoor outdoor there's so many different  covariates and in so many pieces of  information that I think this is a great  opportunity to do model-based Global  Health and try to say what type of an  environment am i entering what type of  information do I have and what is the  most cost effective way to utilize the  tools  as opposed to just being add-on therapy  it's when Bill and Melinda Gates in 2007  announced their goal to eradicate  malaria I was in the room and I remember  thinking now this is it's too hard the  biology you know the transmission is you  know one case leads to a hundred and and  then as I thought about it I realized it  was a paradigm shift right that now you  had to think not about a disease but  actually eradicating a biological  organism and that's a very different  thing and I think that paradigm shift  you see reflected now in the research  and the implementation we are now  focusing much less on disease clearly  disease is important but we're  proceeding much less on disease than we  are on how do we get rid of the organism  and it's transmission as I've been  working with a team and we fund a fair  amount of research the the one question  that we always ask is how will this help  us eradicate malaria and and it's been  interesting to think through that in the  grants that we make and and how we're  approaching all of our work as opposed  to you know thinking like a physician  like I am - okay I see a patient I treat  a patient you've talked a bit about  evaluating the the current tools in  terms of will they contribute to  eradication but where do you see the  holes in the toolbox where or where are  the missing tools is I've been looking  at and trying to evaluate the current  toolbox one of the things that I would  love to see us develop is something that  is less reliant on human behavior  because I think human behavior is so  difficult to change and you know if we  look at at polio there is a human  behavior required for the eradication of  polio you have human behavior on the  part of the vaccinators they have to go  out they have to find everybody on the  planet  the places that they're challenged most  near their their endgame as areas with  social strife and in malaria because of  the vector there may be opportunities to  think about a tool that doesn't require  one human being interacting with another  or one human being taking a medication  for a given period of time  and we know adherence even in the best  situations is challenging you know what  if we could alter the vector with  minimal human behavior  it's an interesting idea you know it  gives the opportunity to think in a  completely different way not focus at  all on the parasite but focus on the  vector I think other things that we're  looking to do is to reduce the reliance  on continuous medication for P vivax can  we go from a 14-day regimen to a single  dose the single dose would be I think  beneficial in multiple different ways  both for treatment because the most  common regimen is a bi be three days in  a row what if you could do that in a  single day you know what would happen to  resistance because you would be you know  getting a full dose and in a single day  as well as thinking about mass Drug  Administration if that's a tool that  would help us eradicate malaria it's a  lot easier to do mass drug  administration if you're giving a single  dose then maybe you have to come back  multiple times so again I think that  also relates to taking the human  behavior factor out of it good in fact  one of the challenges as we talked about  products whether it's genetically  engineering mosquitoes or it's new drugs  or vaccines one of the big issues is the  regulatory issues how do you take things  from discovery and get them approved  particularly in situations where it's a  new idea it's not something  that the industry has done before or or  rarely does and I know that you think  about this a lot and I'm wondering what  your perspective is what the approach  will be one of the things that we try to  do and and are doing more frequently  today is integrated development of which  integrated development includes what is  your regulatory strategy and it's really  hard to plan if you don't know what your  end is going to look like so early  interactions with regulators talking to  them about new products what could these  products be what could the endpoints be  and then developing a strategy on how to  get there in in the most effective way  again remembering that most products  fail so you if you don't know where  you're going and you don't know what the  steps are gonna get there if you do fail  you're likely to fail late as opposed to  failing early which is a very expensive  endeavor being able to make a clear case  as to why you think a product would be  safe and effective or have an  appropriate risk benefit in a given  situation is obviously necessary and  then having the the open discussions  with various regulatory agencies and  sometimes I see us thinking more  developed world regulatory agencies but  what we really need to do is to go to  the communities that we work and get  their perspectives on a risk benefit  mm-hmm I think that's a very important  point you know because it is a different  population that's going to uptake almost  everything that's developed during this  phase of the elimination eradication  campaign  I had a fascinating discussion with one  of my colleagues from Senegal and he was  telling me about malaria in the 1990s  and he was the you know one of the chief  physicians at this hospital and Senegal  and chloroquine resistance hit their  area and he says you know I would sign  2250  certificates a day and I don't have that  experience and a lot of regularly  regulators in developed world don't have  that experience so when you start  thinking about risk and benefit unless  you have that perspective and you've  lived in those communities it's really  hard to have that so that's one of the  things that we're starting to do  especially with novel products is really  get out into the communities where  they're going to be used in and get  their perspectives early on and and try  to have you know whether it's W Joe  stringent regulatory agencies from  developed worlds national regulatory  agencies from countries where it will be  utilized eventually I'll have that  discussion together we've made some  really good progress in that area we've  been working on a project called African  regulatory medicine harmonization which  is thinking taking a regional approach  as to a country approach for product  introduction because most of the  countries don't have a very  well-developed regulatory agency there  are a few but for the most part in  Africa in particular other than South  Africa I think there's relatively little  local expertise in country but from a  regional perspective that could be of  interest we're trying to do multiple  things with this approach so one is to  get a critical mass as you were  describing so we can take the east  african community pull their resources  and their and their best people together  and have them do like dossier reviews  that that's a positive thing the way  works we've have it set up right now is  we're bringing in other regulators like  whether it's swiss medic or folks from  whu-oh or other stringent regulatory  agencies to help answer questions that  they might have and they're also relying  on other stringent regulatory agency  reviews so instead of starting from  scratch they're looking at somebody  else's report ideally and saying okay do  i agree with this report as opposed to  having  through through an entire dossier and  the whole purpose of that is to make  sure you have a quality review and make  sure it's timely but it also benefits  manufacturers I was a manufacturer and  like in my old role and I wanted to  submit a drug to sub-saharan Africa I  would have over 50 dossiers that I would  have to prepare and submit and get  questions back from 50 countries respond  to all 54 a market with very marginal  turn on that well we can create a system  where we have four or five regional  areas and you can drop in four or five  dossiers as opposed to 50 get back four  or five sets of questions and have a  general format that everybody's agreed  to that would dramatically reduce the  the burden on the manufacturers we did a  study about three years ago looking at  the time it takes for a product to go  through the entire regulatory system and  get to people in developing countries in  developed countries it's about 9 to 12  months in developing countries  especially in sub-saharan sub-saharan  Africa we found it's about four to seven  years and it's usually approved in the  developed world goes to whu-oh for  pre-qualification then it goes to the  national regulatory agencies for their  review and approval frequently that none  of those three groups were taking  advantage of prior reviews so they were  doing de novo review each toc de novo  review each time and that obviously  lengthened it by 3x just if you did that  and in in the ideal situation the other  thing that we found is manufacturers  because they had to apply to each  country this the spread of their  submissions of dossiers was sometimes up  to four years right because it had to  fit into their workflow exactly what  we're trying to do through this  harmonization effort  to support mutual reliance and and  minimize I would say non-value-added  activity of applying to each different  country as opposed to regions and we've  had some some great success in East  Africa and and now this approach in this  harmonization effort is moving into West  Africa South Africa already had started  something on their own yet WH o  pre-qualification we found that the drug  side was relying on stringent regulatory  agency reviews but the vaccine side  wasn't and there was about a 13 month  difference in a review time between drug  and vaccines that was a relatively easy  change to do and and and now the  timelines are much better looking at  mutual reliance of national regulatory  agencies on either WH o or other  stringent regulatory agencies reports is  again streamlined it single dossiers as  opposed to multiple so all of these  things have reduced the overall  timelines by close to fifty percent to  what they were three years ago and it  also strikes me that there is an  enormous capacity building potential  here because as as this develops perhaps  the Africans will even be able to do  standalone approval at the highest level  because at some point perhaps that's the  right strategy for registering a product  that's going to be either primarily or  only used in a in a malaria endemic  country I think that really is where we  want to go and the regional approach  makes a sentence to try to stand up 50  plus you know high-quality regulatory  agencies even within the next decade I  think would be almost impossible exactly  and also not productive because malaria  is going to have to be solved on a  regional level you know the disease  doesn't no borders that's something  that's becoming accept  as a as a mode of interaction between  countries what advice would you give to  people early in their career or even pre  career what kinds of things do you think  are important for people to think about  that's a great question I I think that  as scientists and physician scientists  we underestimate the importance of  leadership training I went through 15  years of post high school education and  99% of it was science-based the  leadership component is a totally  different component and and I think it's  one that can be learned for the last  almost 20 years I read some type of  leadership book every quarter I take  time out of my busy schedule to take  some type of course that I think will  help my career development whether it's  you know I've taken courses on  implementation management change  management decision quality a variety of  leadership courses it has really helped  my career and helped me understand what  good leadership looks like and and and  how I can constantly improve myself  I think career development has to start  with the individual nobody is going to  develop your career for you  the sooner I realize that then I could  start taking control over my own career  development understand what some of my  areas of weakness were how am I going to  fix those and and develop that has made  a significant difference in in terms of  how I think how I work early on in my  career I think it was less important but  but today most of what I try to do is  influence my environment mm-hmm and and  certainly I try to do that through  scientific you know discussions and  arguments if you will but a lot of it is  is I think some of the softer skills  whether it's an emotional intelligence  component  or you know how do you think about  decision quality which is something that  we tend to overlook we think we all make  great decisions but you can actually  tell that did you have a quality process  in your decision-making at the time you  make the decision irrespective of  outcome interesting advice because I  agree we we don't spend a lot of time  teaching people how to lead or or  systematically how to make decisions we  know how to test a scientific hypothesis  or follow a protocol but but we don't we  don't focus on how do you get to  decisions and how do you lead people to  decisions well thank you very much it  was a wonder wonderful that you could  spend time with our students thank you
 

